    "ai_tutor_prompt_settings": {
      "answerTypes": [
        "buildJavaScript",
        "documentation"
      ],
      "answerTypeCustomizations": {
        "buildJavaScript": "Follow these rules when generating code:\n- Update the existing selectGig function so it accepts one parameter for the gig name.\n- Update the three button event listeners so each one calls selectGig with the correct gig name as an argument.\n- Do not add additional parameters beyond what the student planned in prompt.md.\n- Do not add loops, arrays, or conditional logic.\n- Keep the code structure and style consistent with what already exists in the file.\n- Do not modify any HTML or CSS.\n- Do not add new functions beyond updating selectGig."
      }
    },
    "bubble_choice_description": "Use AI to build a function that updates the app when a job is selected. Test different inputs and see how the output changes.",
    "long_instructions": "## Do This:\r\n\r\nThe Earnr app lets users pick a food delivery gig and see their projected earnings update. Right now, the function that updates the gig display ignores which gig was clicked — it always shows the same thing. Use AI to add a parameter so the display shows the correct gig.\r\n\r\nPlan in `spec.md`, then prompt the AI with your plan to update the function. Test by clicking different gigs.",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/36df340675efd7016d812cb2d4d89a18-hero.jpg",
    "teacher_markdown": "### **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n**Students may think each button needs its own function**\r\n\r\nSome students may try to create three separate functions—one for burgers, one for tacos, and one for pizza. Encourage them to look for repeated code and ask: **\"What is the same every time a gig is selected, and what information changes?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst selectGig = (gigDescription) => {\r\n  const display = document.getElementById(\"gigDisplay\");\r\n  display.textContent = gigDescription;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students see that one function can handle multiple cases when it receives different inputs.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may add a parameter but forget to pass a value**\r\n\r\nStudents often successfully update the function definition but leave the event listeners unchanged. Help them trace the flow of information by asking: **\"If the function expects a gig description, where does that description come from?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nconst selectGig = (gigDescription) => {\r\n  display.textContent = gigDescription;\r\n};\r\n```\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nburgerBtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\r\n  selectGig(\"Burger combo dropoff\");\r\n});\r\n```\r\n\r\nStudents should recognize that the parameter and the argument work together.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n**Students may accept AI-generated code without checking it against the spec**\r\n\r\nBecause the AI is generating most of the code, some students may focus on whether the code runs rather than whether it matches their plan. Encourage them to test all three buttons and ask: **\"Does each gig display exactly what you wrote in your spec?\"** Remind students that their role is to verify that the AI correctly implemented the requirements."
